    "## Post-lecture quiz"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"dsb-quiz-07-post-1\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>Which library would you use to represent a list of grades of students in class?</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>Numpy</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. Numpy is for multi-dimensional arrays. Pandas is the library for tables of data, like grades in a class.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>SciPy</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. SciPy adds scientific functions. Pandas is the library for tables of data.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>Pandas</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. Pandas handles tables of data, with named columns, like a class list with grades.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"dsb-quiz-07-post-2\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>You have a DataFrame with a list of students, their group number, and average grade. Which operation would you use to compute the average grade per group?</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>average</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. To get a result per group, first group the rows with groupby, then use mean.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>avg</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. The operation that splits rows into groups is groupby. Then mean gives each group's average.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>groupby</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. groupby splits the rows into groups, and mean then gives the average for each group.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"dsb-quiz-07-post-3\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>You have 100 friends and you want to represent the information on how frequently they take pictures with each other. Which data structure would you use?</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>Numpy array</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. Numpy arrays can have many dimensions and hold values of one type, like counts for every pair of friends.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>Pandas DataFrame</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. For numbers that are all one type, a Numpy array is simpler than a DataFrame and has more math operations.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>Pandas Series</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. A Series is a single sequence of values, like a list. Pairs of friends need a multi-dimensional array.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  }
